  "query_preview": "..\n\nArea sensor and camera observations have shown a mix of rain and\nsnow overnight across the CWA with a cold frontal passage. This\nhas also come with gusty northwest winds with the Lake Wind\nAdvisory for Fort Peck Lake in effect through Noon Saturday.\nIsolated wind gusts in excess of 58 mph have been observed over\nthe Little Rockies but will remain small in spatial scale and\nshort in duration, thus have held off on High Wind Warning\nissuance, though those traveling along 191 south of Malta through\nearly Saturday morning should keep in mind the crosswind\npotential.\n\nEnsembles show a broad trough pattern remaining in place with a\nnew trough and closed low setting up across the Pacific NW by the\nearly to middle part of next week which will eventually shift\neastward. The bottom line is this will help maintain below average\ntemperatures across NE Montana over the next several days. Weak\nshortwaves may produce isolated or widely scattered chances for\nprecipitation toward the middle of the week, though widespread\nsignificant precipitation appears unlikely.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nNBM held the line this forecast cycle except for minor adjustments\nto near term pops to match trends and for collaboration. Near term\nwinds were also nudged upward over the Little Rockies which have \ncome in on the higher side of the guidance envelope. Looking \nahead, confidence increasing overall for a quiet start to the week\nwith below normal temperatures, though increasing ensemble spread\nin the 500mb heights is noted by midweek.",
